ntional washing machines useless. The experiments show that laundry **can** be done in microgravity if you: - Contain the water entirely within a sealed bag. - Use detergent that doesn’t need rinsing. - Rely on mechanical action (squishing the bag) instead of tumbling in free air. The real barriers aren’t just physics—they’re logistics and priority: - **Water is too precious:** Even small amounts add up, and the recycling system works best with humidity and urine, not with dirty laundry water which would contain lint, skin cells, and soap residues that can clog filters. - **Energy, volume, and time:** A washing machine would draw power, take up rack space (which is incredibly limited), and cost crew time. Currently, the effort saved isn’t worth it when resupply ships come regularly and can take the trash away. - **Sanitation:** The ISS is small and shared. A smelly gym sock left to dry for reuse just isn’t pleasant when you’re floating right next to it. Simply using an item a few more times then trashing it is simpler. So to summarize: You’re probably recalling the news about the laundry experiments, which have indeed shown it’s **technically feasible**. But operationally, no astronaut currently washes their clothes as a standard activity. They’re still wearing them out and throwing them away. The phrase “impossible” was too strong on my part—better to say it’s not yet implemented due to engineering and resource priorities, not just because water floats.
